## sess120 — blocker 5 landed and the last three unwindowed clears are closed

**Build: VERSION still `0.11.441` (landing incomplete, not revved), srcversion `A78D555EAA0734BC6BBFD6E` → `C3127EAA4E9A2D59061A79D`. Builds clean, zero new warnings. Still STOP-SHIP; fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, for the same reason as sess119: leaving a half-wired clear-window mechanism in `dlm_caw.c` to go work another defect is strictly worse than finishing it.

**Blocker 5 turned out not to need a fallback policy — it needed the allocator removed from the destructive path.** `lreq_clr_begin` allocated a spare entry *before* looking it up, then freed it on the losing race. Making it find-first eliminates the allocation entirely for any resource this node holds a grant on, because `lreq_finish` publishes `tenure[]` and `lreq_gc` refuses to free an entry with nonzero tenure. Unlock and both DIVERG arms therefore never allocate. A 64-entry per-ctx reserve covers the genuinely-absent case, restocked only from allocation-safe contexts, and `lreq_gc` returns quiescent entries to it — so an entry a clear window creates goes straight back when that window closes and the steady-state draw is zero.

There is a second, independent reason it had to change: `mxfs_pal_alloc` is `GFP_KERNEL`. Reclaim entered from inside a DLM release can re-enter XFS writeback, which acquires the very resource being released. That hazard was live on the unlock path.

**The behavioural change is in unlock.** It used to proceed under the legacy releasing mark alone, arguing that stranding the lock is worse than the residual aliasing hole. Those outcomes aren't comparable: a stranded lock is a stall that resolves when the peer re-BASTs, an unlinearized clear strips a bit a local writer is still using. It now returns `-EIO` with the lock held. `force_release_self` — the path that strips our bit from every bitmap in every matching slot — returns `-EAGAIN` instead of "proceeding unlinearized".

**The three audit sites are windowed** through one helper whose fail-closed return is `-EAGAIN`, which all three callers already route into their own bounded retry, so none needed new control flow. The convert DOWNGRADE arm is the consequential one: its CAS sets the new lower bit but strips the old higher one, so `node_held_mode` goes *down* and the subsumption argument that exempts the grant paths runs the other way.

Blockers 1, 2 and 7 remain open, plus the Q3(b) audit note that `lreq_plan` records owed state on every derivation without retraction.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling. The marker stays unwritten.

